"LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data (https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol18/p2616-fu.pdf)\nciteturn29802search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 1.3 years ago; doi:10.14778/3742728.3742752 ... Publication rights licensed to the VLDB Endowment. ... doi:10.14778/3742728.3742752\nLobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data. PVLDB, 18(8): 2616-2625, 2025.  \ndoi:10.14778/3742728.3742752\n\nPVLDB Artifact Availability:  \nThe source code, data, and/or other artifacts have been made available at https://github.com/PKU-DAIR/Hetu.\n\n*Equal contribution.  \n†School of Computer Science & Key Lab of High Confidence Software Technologies (MOE), Peking University  \n‡School of Artificial Intelligence, Shanghai Jiao Tong University  \n§Institute of Computational Social Science, Peking University (Qingdao)  \nThis work is licensed under the Creative Commons BY-NC-ND 4.0 International License. Visit https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ to view a copy of this license. For any use beyond those covered by this license, obtain permission by emailing info@vldb.org. Copyright is held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to the VLDB Endowment.  \nProceedings of the VLDB Endowment, Vol. 18, No. 8 ISSN 2150-8097.  \ndoi:10.14778/3742728.3742752\n\n## 1 INTRODUCTION\nTransformer-based [59] pre-trained models, represented by Large Language Models (LLMs) [46, 47, 58, 65], have fueled an ever-increasing demand for their deployment in various applications such as chatbot assistants [50, 69], machine translation [17, 80], summarization [35, 68], database tuning [31, 77, 78], and more [12, 32, 67, 70]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data | arXiv Science (https://arxiv.science/abs/2509.01193)\nciteturn29802search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last week; Publication date ...     10.14778/3742728.3742752 ... LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data. https://doi.org/10.14778/3742728.3742752\nTo tackle these issues, we develop LobRA, a brand new framework that supports processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training LoRA adapters. Two innovative designs are introduced. Firstly, LobRA deploys the FT replicas (i.e., model replicas for FT) with heterogeneous resource usages and parallel configurations, matching the diverse workloads caused by the sequence length variation. Secondly, for each training step, LobRA takes account of the sequence length skewness and dispatches the training data among the heterogeneous FT replicas to achieve workload balance. We conduct experiments to assess the performance of LobRA, validating that it significantly reduces the GPU seconds required for joint FT by 45.03%-60.67%.\n\n## Explore related subjects\n\ncs.DC\n\nRECORD DETAILS\n\nSource\n    arXiv\nPublication date\n    2025-09-01\nSource updated\n    2025-09-03\nDOI\n    10.14778/3742728.3742752\nReuse permissions\n    http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\n\nMetadata is provided by the original archive. This page does not imply that the source document or image is freely reusable.\n\nSheng Lin, Fangcheng Fu, Haoyang Li, Hao Ge, Xuanyu Wang, Jiawen Niu, Yaofeng Tu, Bin Cui. 2025-09-01. LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data. https://doi.org/10.14778/3742728.3742752\n\nCite the original work for its findings. Save a collection to share your selection of sources.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInstitutional Repository of Peking University: LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data (https://ir.pku.edu.cn/handle/20.500.11897/775183)\nciteturn29802search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Issue Date  | Apr-2025Publisher  | PROCEEDINGS OF THE VLDB ENDOWMENT ... DOI  | 10.14778/3742728.3742752\nNevertheless, through in-depth analysis, we reveal the efficiency of joint FT is dampened by two heterogeneity issues in the training data - the sequence length variation and skewness. To tackle these issues, we develop LobRA, a brand new framework that supports processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training LoRA adapters. Two innovative designs are introduced. Firstly, LobRA deploys the FT replicas (i.e., model replicas for FT) with heterogeneous resource usages and parallel configurations, matching the diverse workloads caused by the sequence length variation. Secondly, for each training step, LobRA takes account of the sequence length skewness and dispatches the training data among the heterogeneous FT replicas to achieve workload balance. We conduct experiments to assess the performance of LobRA, validating that it significantly reduces the GPU seconds required for joint FT by 45.03%-60.67%.\nURI  | http://hdl.handle.net/20.500.11897/775183\nISSN  | 2150-8097\nDOI  | 10.14778/3742728.3742752\nIndexed  | SCI(E)\nAppears in Collections:  | 计算机学院\n高可信软件技术教育部重点实验室\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[PDF] LobRA: Multi-Tenant Fine-Tuning over Heterogeneous Data | CiteDrive (https://www.citedrive.com/en/discovery/lobra-multi-tenant-fine-tuning-over-heterogeneous-data/)\nciteturn29802search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; DOI: 10.14778/3742728.3742752 ISSN: 2150-8097\n\nDOI: 10.14778/3742728.3742752 ISSN: 2150-8097\n\n# LobRA: Multi-Tenant Fine-Tuning over Heterogeneous Data\n\nSheng Lin, Fangcheng Fu, Haoyang Li, Hao Ge, Xuanyu Wang, Jiawen Niu, Yaofeng Tu, Bin Cui\n\nShow PDF Cite\n\nWith the breakthrough of Transformer-based pre-trained models, the demand for fine-tuning (FT) to adapt the base pre-trained models to downstream applications continues to grow, so it is essential for service providers to reduce the cost of processing FT requests. Low-rank adaption (LoRA) is a widely used FT technique that only trains small-scale adapters and keeps the base model unaltered, conveying the possibility of processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training different LoRA adapters with a shared base model.\n\nNevertheless, through in-depth analysis, we reveal the efficiency of joint FT is dampened by two heterogeneity issues in the training data — the sequence length variation and skewness. To tackle these issues, we develop LobRA, a brand new framework that supports processing multiple FT tasks by jointly training LoRA adapters. Two innovative designs are introduced. Firstly, LobRA deploys the FT replicas (i.e., model replicas for FT) with heterogeneous resource usages and parallel configurations, matching the diverse workloads caused by the sequence length variation. Secondly, for each training step, LobRA takes account of the sequence length skewness and dispatches the training data among the heterogeneous FT replicas to achieve workload balance. We conduct experiments to assess the performance of LobRA, validating that it significantly reduces the GPU seconds required for joint FT by 45.03%-60.67%.\n\nRelease Year:\n\nISSN:2150-8097\n\nDOI:10.14778/3742728.3742752\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data | Paper (https://rmarcus.info/dbscholar/papers/h869c3134cf070fff)\nciteturn29802search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;     10.14778/3742728.3742752 ...             author = {Lin, Sheng and Fu, Fangcheng and Li, Haoyang and Ge, Hao and Wang, Xuanyu and Niu, Jiawen and Tu, Yaofeng and Cui, Bin},            journal = {PVLDB}, ...             doi = {10.14778/3742728.3742752},\n\n# LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data\n\nSummary: LobRA enables multi-tenant joint fine-tuning of LoRA adapters by tackling two data heterogeneities—sequence-length variation and skew—that hurt joint FT efficiency. It runs heterogeneous FT replicas (varying resource/parallel configs) plus a skew-aware dispatcher to balance per-step workloads, cutting GPU-seconds by ~45–60.7%. (summarized by gpt-5-mini on Feb 09 2026)\n\nPaper ID\n    h869c3134cf070fff\nVenue\n    VLDB\nYear\n    2025\nPagerank\n    4.9793485e-05\nOverall Rank\n    11,282 | 24.15%\nDOI\n    10.14778/3742728.3742752\n\n## Authors\n\n  * 1. Sheng Lin (Peking University)\n  * 2. Fangcheng Fu (Shanghai Jiao Tong University)\n  * 3. Haoyang Li (Peking University)\n  * 4. Hao Ge (Peking University)\n  * 5. Xuanyu Wang (Peking University)\n  * 6. Jiawen Niu (Peking University)\n  * 7. Yaofeng Tu (ZTE Corporation)\n  * 8. Bin Cui (Peking University)\n\n## BibTeX Citation\n\nCopy BibTeX\n    \n    `@article{lin_vldb25,\n            title = {{LobRA: Multi-tenant Fine-tuning over Heterogeneous Data}},\n            author = {Lin, Sheng and Fu, Fangcheng and Li, Haoyang and Ge, Hao and Wang, Xuanyu and Niu, Jiawen and Tu, Yaofeng and Cui, Bin},\n            journal = {PVLDB},\n            series = {{VLDB} '25},\n            volume = {18},\n            number = {8},\n            pages = {2616--2625},\n            doi = {10.14778/3742728.3742752},\n            url = {https://doi.org/10.14778/3742728.3742752},\n            year = {2025}\n    }`\n\n## Incoming Citations (Sorted by Pagerank)\n\nShowing 1 of 1 citing papers.\n\nRank  | Citing Paper  | Year  | Venue  | Pagerank\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n10,577  | Hydraulis: Balancing Large Transformer Model Training via Co-designing Parallel Strategies and Data Assignment  | 2026  | SIGMOD  | 4.9793485e-05\n\nPrevious Page 1 / 1 Next\n\n## Outgoing Citations (Sorted by Pagerank)\n\nShowing 9 of 9 cited papers.\n\nCitations counted here include only citations to other VLDB/SIGMOD/CIDR/PODS papers in this database.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-09-03&page=45&range=31)\nciteturn29802search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; ### Date Published ... @misc{2509.01236, author = {Hao Yang and Zhiyu Yang